# =============================================================================
# fac omnia … dum: a caught error ends the loop
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `fac omnia { … } cape err { … } dum cond` has ordinary host loop control,
#     but in runner/device execution deferred recoverable kernel errors are
#     delivered to this owning handler at per-iteration delivery points
#   • `cape` wraps the whole loop, so a delivered error transfers to its handler
#     and the `dum` test is not reached; host reads can observe errors earlier
#   • this example's synchronous host `iace` is caught before the test; it does
#     not itself exercise deferred kernel-error delivery
#   • the modifier round-trips the formatter
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting the loop to continue after this handler — it wraps the whole
#     `fac … dum`, so a caught error ends it; to keep looping, catch inside the
#     body of an outer `dum` loop (see fac-omnia-dum-continue)
#
# See also: fac, cape, omnia, dum
# =============================================================================

# fac omnia … dum — the handler ends the loop
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   facStmt :← 'fac' 'omnia'? block cape_clause? ('dum' expr)?
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   iteratio 1 / iteratio 2 / iteratio 3 / interceptum / finis 3
#
# BACKEND:
#   Runner/device: deferred recoverable kernel errors are delivered at each
#   iteration's delivery points; a handler around the whole loop ends it when
#   it catches one. In this host-only example, `iace` is synchronous and reaches
#   `cape` before the condition test.

incipit {
    varia numerus i ← 0
    fac omnia {
        i ← i + 1
        nota "iteratio §"(i)
        si i ≥ 3 {
            iace "tertia"
        }
    }
    cape err {
        nota "interceptum"
    } dum i ≺ 10
    nota "finis §"(i)
}
